Woodgrove Bank is building a network-secured Foundry resource with its own virtual network so that hosted agents run with no public egress. The network team wrote this Bicep for the agent subnet. What must change for the subnet to be supported?<pre>resource agentSubnet 'Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/subnets@2024-05-01' = {
  parent: vnet
  name: 'snet-agents'
  properties: {
    addressPrefix: '10.20.4.0/28'
    delegations: [
      {
        name: 'agents'
        properties: { serviceName: 'Microsoft.Web/serverFarms' }
      }
    ]
  }
}</pre>

Litware's HR agent is created with the code below. The <code>hr-policies</code> index has searchable text fields, a searchable <code>Collection(Edm.Single)</code> field and a semantic configuration. How does the tool query the index when <code>query_type</code> and <code>top_k</code> are not set?
<pre>AzureAISearchTool(
    azure_ai_search=AzureAISearchToolResource(
        indexes=[AISearchIndexResource(
            project_connection_id=connection_id,
            index_name="hr-policies",
        )]
    )
)</pre>

Tailwind Traders hosts a support chatbot on a model deployment that uses a custom guardrail. A tester sends a prompt that tries to replace the assistant's system persona. The model answers it, and the response carries this annotation:
<pre>"prompt_filter_results": [{
  "prompt_index": 0,
  "content_filter_results": {
    "jailbreak": { "filtered": false, "detected": true }
  }
}]</pre>
Which change to the guardrail makes the next such prompt be stopped before the model processes it?

Northwind Traders loads contracts into an Azure AI Search index with a nightly blob indexer that feeds a Foundry IQ knowledge source. Users report that about 60 PDFs never appear in answers. The on-call engineer calls Get Indexer Status (<code>api-version=2026-04-01</code>) and gets this trimmed response:
<pre>{
  "name": "contracts-indexer",
  "status": "running",
  "lastResult": {
    "status": "success",
    "itemsProcessed": 940,
    "itemsFailed": 60,
    "errors": [ ... ],
    "warnings": []
  }
}</pre>
Which interpretation is correct?

Woodgrove Bank fronts a provisioned (PTU) deployment and a Standard deployment of the same model with Azure API Management. The PTU deployment must be used first, and the Standard deployment only when the PTU deployment can't take more requests. The team created this pool, but during peaks clients receive 429 responses from the PTU deployment while the Standard deployment sits idle:<pre>resource pool 'Microsoft.ApiManagement/service/backends@2023-09-01-preview' = {
  name: 'myAPIM/gpt-pool'
  properties: {
    type: 'Pool'
    pool: {
      services: [
        {
          id: ptuBackend.id
          priority: 1
        }
        {
          id: standardBackend.id
          priority: 2
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}</pre>What should the team add?

Tailwind Traders extracts the vendor, topics and a short summary from supplier emails with a Chat Completions call that must use strict structured outputs. The request below is not valid for strict mode. Which <b>THREE</b> changes are required?<pre>"response_format": {
  "type": "json_schema",
  "json_schema": {
    "name": "supplier_email",
    "strict": true,
    "schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "vendor": { "type": "string" },
        "topics": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" }, "maxItems": 5 },
        "summary": { "type": ["string", "null"] }
      },
      "required": ["vendor", "topics"]
    }
  }
}</pre>

Tailwind Traders' catalog tool must replace only the sofa in <code>living-room.png</code> (1536 &times; 1024 pixels) by calling a <code>gpt-image-2</code> deployment. The developer wrote the request below and now has to produce <code>mask.png</code>. Which mask meets the documented requirements?
<pre>files = {
    "image": ("living-room.png", open("living-room.png", "rb"), "image/png"),
    "mask": ("mask.png", open("mask.png", "rb"), "image/png"),
}
data = {"prompt": "Replace the sofa with a green velvet sofa", "n": 1, "size": "1536x1024"}
requests.post(edit_url, headers={"api-key": key}, data=data, files=files)</pre>
